## Governing foundation

This change is constrained by existing authority, especially:

- `architecture/decisions/ADR-0006-eos-sovereignty-and-external-sdlc-assimilation.md`;
- `engineering/changes/CR-0001.md`;
- `engineering/changes/CR-0002.md`;
- `governance/canonical-state-model.md`;
- `governance/authority.md`;
- `governance/decision-process.md`;
- `governance/planning-engine.md`;
- `governance/policy-engine.md`;
- `governance/execution-engine.md`;
- `engineering/lifecycle/README.md`;
- `engineering/definition-of-ready.md`;
- `product/product-requirements.md`.

ADR-0006 remains authoritative and is not superseded by this change. Its decision meaning MUST remain intact.

### Product requirements

Refine existing requirements that already own the relevant concerns, expected to include:

- FR-007 — bounded agent context;
- FR-012 — memory/intelligence/execution loop;
- FR-013 — anti-hallucination engineering memory;
- FR-014 — dependency-aware agent work;
- FR-015 — progressive autonomy;
- FR-016 — cross-harness review;
- FR-023 — declarative governance/change control/audit;
- FR-024 — operational and execution observability;
- QR-008 — progressive trust;
- QR-010 — auditability.

Add one product requirement for the currently missing concern, expected to be:

- `FR-037 — Provide Adaptive AI-Driven Engineering Workflow Planning`.

The new requirement will require an inspectable proposed engineering pathway derived from governed intent, repository context, lifecycle state, risk, complexity, dependencies, outstanding decisions, evidence, and policy while preserving mandatory EOS gates and authority.

### Specifications

Establish an initial AI-engineering specification tranche, expected to include:

- `FUN-AIENG-0001 — Adaptive Engineering Workflow`;
- `IFC-AIENG-0001 — Engineering Agent Contract`;
- `SEC-AIENG-0001 — Autonomy, Authority, and Approval Gates`.

The exact paths and IDs remain subject to repository verification and specification conventions at the time of creation.

## Relationship to external AI-driven development models

External AI-driven development frameworks are design donors and compatibility targets only.

Useful concepts MAY be assimilated, including:

- AI-initiated workflow analysis;
- AI-generated plans;
- proactive clarification;
- adaptive workflow breadth and depth;
- human disposition of consequential decisions;
- bounded execution after authorization;
- persistent governed context;
- end-to-end traceability;
- explicit verification and review.

External lifecycle names, task hierarchies, dependency graphs, approval stores, evidence stores, canonical state, or vendor-specific steering mechanisms MUST NOT become competing Monad authority.

## Required conformance scenarios

The normative tranche must define or trace acceptance behavior for at least:

1. a small low-risk reversible change receiving a lightweight pathway;
2. ambiguous product intent requiring an explicit decision instead of AI guessing;
3. architecture change discovered during execution routing through EOSC;
4. bounded autonomous execution of an explicitly authorized low-risk Work Packet;
5. security-sensitive work increasing required rigor and authority;
6. independent review that cannot be satisfied by trivial executor self-review;
7. governing-input drift invalidating a stale execution contract;
8. AI provider failure without corruption of canonical engineering state;
9. an authorized human denial causing replanning rather than repeated pressure for the rejected outcome;
10. verification evidence invalidating an assumption and routing to replanning/change control.

## Expected machine impact

No immediate machine-state or product-runtime implementation is authorized by this Change Request.

After normative design stabilizes, later separately authorized implementation may determine that changes are required to:

- deterministic policy predicates;
- context/execution/review contract schemas;
- adaptive-pathway representation;
- plan/context/governing-input fingerprints;
- provider-neutral executor/harness abstraction;
- decision/approval gate checks;
- review-independence checks;
- observability records;
- conformance fixtures;
- CLI operations.

Those changes are not pre-authorized merely by appearing in this list. Existing models SHALL be extended before new infrastructure is invented.

## Validation required before implementation planning

Before implementation Work Packets are authorized, the normative tranche should demonstrate that:

- ADR-0006 remains intact and authoritative;
- no second lifecycle or current-state authority has been introduced;
- AI initiative and binding authority are explicitly distinct;
- the adaptive pathway preserves mandatory EOS gates;
- material ambiguity can block only affected work and route to native decision semantics;
- requirement/specification traceability is complete;
- executor/reviewer independence can be expressed without provider lock-in;
- stale governing inputs invalidate execution authority;
- raw transcripts are not required as canonical state;
- provider failure does not invalidate canonical engineering state;
- all required conformance scenarios have a normative owner;
- existing MVP scope and current authorized Work Packets remain unchanged;
- repository integrity and EOS strict verification pass after any later machine-level implementation.
